<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wanli Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1563">1563</a>–<a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Zhu Yijun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wanli Emperor was the 14th emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, reigning from 1572 to 1620, the longest reign of the dynasty. His early years saw the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-juzheng">Zhang Juzheng</a> and costly wars, including the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> against the Japanese invasions of the 1590s. Later he withdrew from government for decades, leaving offices vacant and the bureaucracy paralysed, which historians see as a cause of Ming decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor.jpg" alt="Wanli Emperor" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MingShenzong1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Yijun was born on 4 September 1563, during the reign of his grandfather, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor">Jiajing Emperor</a>. His father, Zhu Zaiji, was the heir to the throne, and his mother, Lady Li, was one of his concubines. His father became the Longqing Emperor in 1567 and named him heir in 1568. When the Longqing Emperor died on 5 July 1572, Zhu Yijun succeeded two weeks later, on 19 July, at the age of nine, and adopted the era name Wanli, meaning &quot;ten thousand years (of rule)&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>For the first ten years Grand Secretary Zhang Juzheng effectively led the government, supported by the Emperor's mother and the eunuch Feng Bao. He centralised government, streamlined administration and strengthened the military, and the efficiency of administration improved markedly between 1572 and 1582. After Zhang died in 1582 the Emperor changed his administrative arrangements. From 1586 a dispute over the succession divided the bureaucracy: the Emperor favoured his third son, Zhu Changxun, the son of his consort Lady Zheng, while many officials upheld the claim of his eldest son, Zhu Changluo, who was named heir only in 1601.</p><p>In the 1590s the Ming put down a rebellion at Ningxia in 1592, drove the Japanese out of most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> by 1593 and thwarted a second Japanese invasion in 1597. In 1596 a shortage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> after the costly rebuilding of palaces burnt in a fire led the Emperor to send <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and officers to open mines, which local authorities opposed. In 1596 he also tried to set up a parallel administration of eunuchs, abandoned in 1606. The Donglin Academy, founded in 1604, became an intellectual centre critical of the court. In 1619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> defeated Ming armies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sarhu">Battle of Sarhu</a> and took part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. After 1588 the Emperor no longer left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Emperor's health failed in 1620, and he died on 18 August. An edict the next day sent a million liang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> to the frontier troops and ordered an end to mining and trade dues. His eldest son, Zhu Changluo, took the throne on 28 August as the Taichang Emperor. Traditional Chinese historiography portrays the Wanli Emperor as a main cause of the dynasty's decline, citing greed, misuse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> power and seclusion. Confucian scholars' hostility came partly from his protection of military officers against complaints from civilian officials.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wanli Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 16th century, the long withdrawal of the <b>Wanli Emperor</b> from government, eunuch influence and factional struggles such as those between the Donglin faction and its opponents had weakened the bureaucracy, while the costs of border defence and of the Korean war strained state finances.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Wanli Emperor</b> (r. 1572–1620) began with able advisers, among them the Grand Secretary Zhang Juzheng, but grew tired of his quarrelling ministers and stayed behind the walls of the Forbidden City, where officials had to bribe powerful eunuchs to have their messages carried to him.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1600" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7418 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wanli Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1600">What was here in 1600? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
